WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:03.930
A phone book.

2
00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:07.095
Given a name, a hash map finds the number

3
00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:09.020
almost instantly.

4
00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:11.139
But ask a different question.

5
00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.991
Who is the last person who

6
00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:14.860
comes before Morgan?

7
00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:19.410
Or: list everyone from M to P, in order.

8
00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:22.530
A hash map has no idea of order.

9
00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:23.650
To answer

10
00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:25.870
it would have to look at every entry.

11
00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:29.120
We need a map that keeps its keys sorted.

12
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:31.900
This is the sorted map.

13
00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:34.420
We'll see the operations it offers

14
00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:36.620
how a balanced tree answers them

15
00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:39.930
and why a sorted list is not a substitute.

16
00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:43.520
Besides get, put, and remove

17
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:46.020
a sorted map has first and last

18
00:00:46.020 --> 00:00:47.990
the smallest and biggest keys.

19
00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:51.500
floor of a key is the entry with the greatest

20
00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:54.447
key that is at most the key.

21
00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:56.791
ceiling is the entry with the smallest

22
00:00:56.850 --> 00:00:59.100
key that is at least the key.

23
00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:01.440
And a range query

24
00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:03.940
all entries with keys from low

25
00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:06.130
up to but not including high.

26
00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.165
And of course you can walk

27
00:01:08.230 --> 00:01:10.960
through all the entries, in sorted order.

28
00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:13.950
Here are six exam scores

29
00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:14.950
with their students.

30
00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:16.950
The first is sixty-one

31
00:01:16.950 --> 00:01:19.180
and the last is ninety-five.

32
00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:22.638
floor of eighty: no one scored eighty

33
00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:24.665
so the greatest score that

34
00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:27.100
is at most eighty.

35
00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.930
That is seventy-nine, Eli.

36
00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:33.066
ceiling of eighty is the smallest

37
00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:34.990
score that is at least eighty.

38
00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:37.120
That is eighty-four, Fay.

39
00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:39.488
The range from seventy up to

40
00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:42.260
ninety gives four entries: seventy-two

41
00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:45.980
seventy-nine, eighty-four, and eighty-eight.

42
00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.310
Ninety itself would be excluded.

43
00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:51.020
How does a tree find the floor?

44
00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:53.490
Walk down from the root, as in a search.

45
00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.190
When a key is at most the target

46
00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:58.417
remember it as a candidate, and go right

47
00:01:58.470 --> 00:01:59.741
because a better candidate

48
00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:01.270
can only be bigger.

49
00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:04.040
When a key is too big, go left.

50
00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:05.920
When you run out of tree

51
00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:08.580
the last candidate is the answer.

52
00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.030
That is one path, so about log n steps.

53
00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.010
A range query is similar.

54
00:02:14.010 --> 00:02:15.404
Go through the keys in order

55
00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:17.389
but skip every subtree that lies

56
00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:19.210
completely outside the range.

57
00:02:19.210 --> 00:02:21.070
That costs log n

58
00:02:21.070 --> 00:02:23.940
plus one step for each entry returned.

59
00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:27.050
Why not keep two sorted lists

60
00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:29.410
one for keys and one for values?

61
00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:32.330
Binary search finds a key in log n steps

62
00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:35.560
and floor, ceiling, and ranges work too.

63
00:02:35.910 --> 00:02:38.020
The catch is insertion.

64
00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:39.743
A new key goes in the middle

65
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:41.697
and everything after it has to

66
00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:43.590
shift one place to the right.

67
00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:46.530
On average, half of the list moves.

68
00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:49.910
So one insertion costs n over two moves

69
00:02:49.910 --> 00:02:52.460
instead of log n comparisons.

70
00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:54.430
Pause and predict.

71
00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:56.590
We insert ten thousand random keys

72
00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:59.760
one at a time, into two sorted lists.

73
00:03:00.110 --> 00:03:02.480
How many entries do the shifts move

74
00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:03.620
in total?

75
00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:04.860
About ten thousand?

76
00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:06.460
About a hundred thousand?

77
00:03:06.460 --> 00:03:08.510
About twenty-five million?

78
00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:11.170
About twenty-five million.

79
00:03:11.170 --> 00:03:13.788
Exactly, twenty-four million nine hundred

80
00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:15.770
nine thousand forty-three.

81
00:03:15.770 --> 00:03:18.283
The tree, for the same keys

82
00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:20.583
made about one hundred twenty-two

83
00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:22.420
thousand comparisons.

84
00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.650
But there is a case where the list wins.

85
00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:28.430
If the keys arrive already in order

86
00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:30.130
every new key goes at the end

87
00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:31.810
and nothing has to shift.

88
00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:33.080
Zero moves.

89
00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:35.160
The tree needs about the same

90
00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:36.660
work whatever the order

91
00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:38.571
about one hundred twenty-four thousand

92
00:03:38.620 --> 00:03:40.720
comparisons for ten thousand keys

93
00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:44.230
and it stays balanced, with fourteen levels.

94
00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:47.341
So: a sorted list for data that

95
00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:50.242
arrives in order, or hardly ever changes.

96
00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.865
A balanced tree when keys come

97
00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:54.420
and go in any order.

98
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:56.430
To sum up.

99
00:03:56.430 --> 00:04:00.490
A sorted map adds first, last, floor

100
00:04:00.490 --> 00:04:01.150
ceiling

101
00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:03.960
and range queries to the ordinary map.

102
00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:07.360
A balanced search tree, whether AVL

103
00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.200
two-four, or red-black

104
00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:12.350
answers them all in log n time.

105
00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:15.410
A sorted list answers them too

106
00:04:15.410 --> 00:04:18.300
but pays n over two on every insertion.

107
00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:20.540
That ends the search trees.

108
00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:23.260
In the next chapter, we give up order

109
00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:26.620
and see how a hash table gets constant time.

110
00:04:26.620 --> 00:04:28.010
Now, the code.

111
00:04:28.637 --> 00:04:31.267
Here is the sorted map in Python.

112
00:04:31.267 --> 00:04:33.507
We'll build it on our AVL tree

113
00:04:33.507 --> 00:04:35.837
and compare it with the bisect module.

114
00:04:36.417 --> 00:04:39.067
Sorted Map wraps an AVL tree.

115
00:04:39.067 --> 00:04:41.737
put, get, and remove just call the tree.

116
00:04:42.087 --> 00:04:45.257
first entry walks left as far as it can

117
00:04:45.257 --> 00:04:48.047
and last entry walks right.

118
00:04:48.627 --> 00:04:51.577
floor entry is a walk down from the root.

119
00:04:51.577 --> 00:04:53.947
If the key is equal, we are done.

120
00:04:54.297 --> 00:04:56.347
If the node's key is smaller

121
00:04:56.347 --> 00:04:57.687
it is a candidate

122
00:04:57.687 --> 00:05:00.207
and a closer one can only be on the right.

123
00:05:00.207 --> 00:05:01.537
Otherwise, go left.

124
00:05:01.887 --> 00:05:04.207
ceiling entry is the mirror image.

125
00:05:04.787 --> 00:05:07.577
sub map collects the keys from low

126
00:05:07.577 --> 00:05:09.907
up to but not including high.

127
00:05:10.257 --> 00:05:12.786
It only visits the left side if low

128
00:05:12.867 --> 00:05:15.095
is smaller than the node's key

129
00:05:15.167 --> 00:05:17.213
and the right side if the node's

130
00:05:17.267 --> 00:05:18.967
key is smaller than high.

131
00:05:18.967 --> 00:05:21.337
So whole subtrees are skipped.

132
00:05:21.917 --> 00:05:23.687
Run it on the exam scores.

133
00:05:23.687 --> 00:05:26.867
In order: sixty-one, seventy-two

134
00:05:26.867 --> 00:05:30.187
seventy-nine, eighty-four, eighty-eight

135
00:05:30.187 --> 00:05:31.357
ninety-five.

136
00:05:31.707 --> 00:05:34.297
floor of eighty is seventy-nine.

137
00:05:34.297 --> 00:05:36.505
ceiling is eighty-four.

138
00:05:36.597 --> 00:05:38.462
The range from seventy to

139
00:05:38.537 --> 00:05:40.317
ninety has four entries.

140
00:05:40.317 --> 00:05:43.227
And get of eighty gives None.

141
00:05:43.807 --> 00:05:46.177
The standard library has bisect

142
00:05:46.177 --> 00:05:48.497
which keeps a plain list sorted.

143
00:05:48.497 --> 00:05:51.097
Bisect Map uses two parallel lists

144
00:05:51.097 --> 00:05:53.887
and bisect left to find the position.

145
00:05:54.237 --> 00:05:55.720
Lookups are fast.

146
00:05:55.807 --> 00:05:57.956
But the list insert method shifts

147
00:05:58.027 --> 00:06:00.247
everything after the position

148
00:06:00.247 --> 00:06:02.237
and we count those moves.

149
00:06:02.587 --> 00:06:04.975
For ten thousand random keys

150
00:06:05.057 --> 00:06:08.270
the lists moved twenty-four million nine hundred

151
00:06:08.337 --> 00:06:10.766
nine thousand forty-three entries

152
00:06:10.837 --> 00:06:12.645
and the tree made about one hundred

153
00:06:12.697 --> 00:06:14.737
twenty-two thousand comparisons.

154
00:06:14.737 --> 00:06:17.787
Sorted input needs no moves at all.

155
00:06:18.367 --> 00:06:20.410
But moves are fast

156
00:06:20.517 --> 00:06:21.946
because Python shifts a list

157
00:06:21.997 --> 00:06:24.037
with a single memory copy.

158
00:06:24.037 --> 00:06:25.777
Here is the stopwatch

159
00:06:25.777 --> 00:06:28.047
measured once on this machine.

160
00:06:28.397 --> 00:06:31.207
At a thousand keys, the list is faster.

161
00:06:31.207 --> 00:06:33.747
At ten thousand, still faster.

162
00:06:33.747 --> 00:06:37.027
At a hundred thousand, about even.

163
00:06:37.027 --> 00:06:40.047
At three hundred thousand, the tree wins

164
00:06:40.047 --> 00:06:42.517
about six seconds, against nine.

165
00:06:42.867 --> 00:06:45.157
Our Python tree is not fast

166
00:06:45.157 --> 00:06:47.597
it is all objects and recursion.

167
00:06:47.597 --> 00:06:49.977
The shape of the growth is what matters

168
00:06:49.977 --> 00:06:51.547
and it keeps growing.

169
00:06:52.127 --> 00:06:54.787
The test runs random puts and removes

170
00:06:54.857 --> 00:06:56.396
two hundred operations on

171
00:06:56.457 --> 00:06:58.167
each of forty maps.

172
00:06:58.517 --> 00:07:01.607
After each one, it picks a random probe

173
00:07:01.607 --> 00:07:03.946
and compares floor, ceiling

174
00:07:04.027 --> 00:07:06.617
and sub map with the answers from the

175
00:07:06.687 --> 00:07:08.687
bisect module on a sorted list.

176
00:07:08.687 --> 00:07:10.757
They must all agree.

177
00:07:11.337 --> 00:07:12.589
Common mistakes.

178
00:07:12.667 --> 00:07:14.612
Keeping a dict and sorting

179
00:07:14.687 --> 00:07:16.627
its keys for every query.

180
00:07:16.627 --> 00:07:19.857
That costs n log n each time.

181
00:07:20.207 --> 00:07:22.977
Mixing up floor and lower.

182
00:07:22.977 --> 00:07:26.447
Floor allows an equal key, lower does not.

183
00:07:26.797 --> 00:07:29.147
Off-by-one at the end of a range.

184
00:07:29.147 --> 00:07:32.427
Ours includes low and excludes high

185
00:07:32.427 --> 00:07:34.637
the same convention as Python slices.

186
00:07:34.987 --> 00:07:36.614
And using bisect on a list

187
00:07:36.677 --> 00:07:38.217
that is not sorted.

188
00:07:38.217 --> 00:07:39.797
It will not complain.

189
00:07:39.797 --> 00:07:41.827
It will just return nonsense.

190
00:07:42.407 --> 00:07:44.177
In the standard library

191
00:07:44.177 --> 00:07:46.337
Python has no sorted map.

192
00:07:46.337 --> 00:07:48.897
For a small or mostly-read map

193
00:07:48.897 --> 00:07:51.407
use a sorted list with bisect.

194
00:07:51.757 --> 00:07:53.572
For a big, changing one

195
00:07:53.647 --> 00:07:55.819
the third-party sortedcontainers

196
00:07:55.887 --> 00:07:57.947
package gives a Sorted Dict.

197
00:07:57.947 --> 00:08:00.437
In Java, tree map does it all.

198
00:08:00.787 --> 00:08:03.257
That completes balanced search trees.

199
00:08:03.257 --> 00:08:04.787
Next, hashing.

